Here is a stepwise systematic automation:

Step 1: Initiate the celestial_body_database and populate it with up-to-date positions, masses, and orbital information.

Step 2: Using physics_calculator, compute the gravitational force exerted on a spacecraft navigating between any two celestial bodies. 

Step3: Calculate potential gravitational assists using trajectory_planner. 

Step4: For each trajectory calculate the additional velocity that can be achieved due to the gravitational assist. 

Step5: Then calculate total energy (kinetic + potential) required for the mission.

Step6: Input mission details (start point, end point, allowed velocity changes, fuel capacity) into fuel_optimizer to estimate minimum fuel requirements for chosen trajectory.

Step7: Run mission_simulator for each trajectory to get an understanding of any unforeseen parameters or potential anomalies that may not be accounted in the initial planning.

Step8: Take 1% worst-case scenarios for all mission parameters and calculate fuel requirements on the basis of them to get an upper-bound estimate for required fuel.

Step9: Analyse the lower and upper-bound fuel requirements to come up with an optimal mission plan.

